      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are correct that a one-sided approach is unconvincing in a high-stakes debate; a more winning position argues that \"absolutism on one end destroys absolutism on the other.\" While the President needs a \"operational bubble\" for candid counsel, this zone cannot expand until it encroaches on the definitive duty of the legislature to conduct oversight or the judiciary to discover truth based on *United States v. Nixon*.\n\nTherefore, your strongest legal narrative should claim that while the executive has some privilege to delegate and block irrelevant demands, unlimited immunity from congressional subpoenas is a clear violation of the constitutional structure of checks and balances. By arguing that the \"zone of privacy\" must be strictly limited by its destructiveness to the other branches, you can concede minor protective needs while firmly asserting that the *modern expansion* of the power is a dangerous and unconstitutional overreach.",
